<Project>

  <!--
    Everything that has to READ a property the project itself sets lives here rather than in a
    Directory.Build.props. The SDK imports the .props file at the TOP of a project, before the
    project's own PropertyGroups have been evaluated, so a condition on $(ReleaseTrain) there
    would always see an empty value. This file is imported at the BOTTOM, after the project body,
    which is the only place the declaration below can be observed.

    This file is the whole build-side contract of the release pipeline. It deliberately does NOT
    set anything else — target frameworks, analyzers, warning levels, central package management —
    because none of that is a release concern and all of it is the repository's to decide when the
    first project lands.
  -->

  <!--
    <ReleaseTrain> is how a project joins a release train, and it is the ONLY place that membership
    is recorded: tools/trains.sh discovers the projects of a train by reading this property out of
    the project files, and tools/packaging/pack.sh packs exactly what it finds.

    Declaring a train is therefore a statement that the project SHIPS, which is what makes it
    packable. Stating it here rather than in each project keeps the two facts from disagreeing — a
    project declaring a train but left unpackable would be silently absent from its own release.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''">
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Ship symbols as a separate .snupkg so the main package stays lean but stays debuggable.

    Excluded for a tool: PackAsTool produces a self-contained tool package and no symbol package,
    so asking for one there produces a pack warning and nothing else. Both workflows already expect
    a train to ship no .snupkg (`shopt -s nullglob` before globbing the assets), so the cli train
    passing through here without symbols is the case they were written for.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != '' and '$(PackAsTool)' != 'true'">
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Deterministic, path-normalized builds on CI; a no-op for local builds. This is what makes
       "recompiling the same source at the same version reproduces the same bytes" true, which is
       the assumption pack.sh relies on when it compiles what it packs. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Microsoft.Sbom.Targets is what makes -p:GenerateSBOM=true embed an SPDX inventory
    (_manifest/spdx_2.2/manifest.spdx.json) in the produced package; pack.sh asserts the manifest is
    present in every .nupkg it builds, so a project that shipped without this reference would fail
    the pack rather than publish an inventory-less package.

    Attached to the train declaration rather than repeated in every publishable project: the two
    always go together, and a new project would otherwise ship without an SBOM until somebody
    noticed. Build-time only (PrivateAssets=all): it never reaches a consumer's dependency graph.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Sbom.Targets" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The version pin, supplied here only while central package management is NOT in use. Under CPM a
    Version on a PackageReference is an error (NU1008) and the pin belongs in Directory.Packages.props
    instead — so the day this repository adopts CPM, this ItemGroup goes quiet on its own and the
    version moves, rather than the build breaking on a conflict nobody predicted.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != '' and '$(ManagePackageVersionsCentrally)' != 'true'">
    <PackageReference Update="Microsoft.Sbom.Targets" Version="4.1.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The package version and the assembly version must say the same thing.

    They are two independent properties, and nothing in the SDK ties them: $(Version) names the
    package, $(AssemblyVersion) is stamped into the binary and is what the CLR binds on. A release
    that sets one and not the other produces a package whose number is right and whose assembly
    identity is not — which no amount of building, testing or packing notices, because every one of
    those steps is internally consistent with itself. A sibling repository shipped exactly that: a
    package numbered 1.0.0 around an assembly stamped 0.0.0.0, and a consumer met a
    FileNotFoundException naming a version that was published and does not exist.

    Be clear about what this does NOT catch. This compares two PROPERTIES of one evaluation, so it
    sees a hand-set AssemblyVersion disagreeing at the source — not a pack over STALE bytes, where
    both properties agree and the assembly on disk was stamped by an earlier compilation. What
    closes that hole is pack.sh compiling what it packs; this target is the second line.

    Both entry points are covered so the check holds wherever the stamp is decided: CoreCompile is
    where it is applied, and GenerateNuspec catches a pack invoked on its own.

    Only the three leading components are compared. A four-part $(AssemblyVersion) against a
    three-part $(Version) is the ordinary case, not a mismatch, and a SemVer pre-release label
    (1.0.0-rc.1) is deliberately outside the comparison: the CLR has nowhere to put it, so the
    assembly of a pre-release legitimately reads 1.0.0.

    A version neither side can parse as three numbers leaves this quiet rather than guessing. That
    is a real gap and a small one — release.yml already refuses any tag that is not SemVer, so the
    shape reaching a release is always one this reads.
  -->
  <Target Name="KillMutantsVerifyAssemblyVersionMatchesPackageVersion"
          Condition="'$(ReleaseTrain)' != ''"
          BeforeTargets="CoreCompile;GenerateNuspec">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_KmPackageVersionCore>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match('$(Version)', '^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+').Value)</_KmPackageVersionCore>
      <_KmAssemblyVersionCore>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match('$(AssemblyVersion)', '^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+').Value)</_KmAssemblyVersionCore>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Error Condition="'$(_KmPackageVersionCore)' != '' and '$(_KmAssemblyVersionCore)' != '' and '$(_KmPackageVersionCore)' != '$(_KmAssemblyVersionCore)'"
           Code="KMPACK001"
           Text="$(PackageId) would ship version $(Version) with an assembly stamped $(AssemblyVersion). A consumer binds on the assembly version, so the two must agree on their first three components. Set Version (the release passes -p:Version) rather than AssemblyVersion, and do not set AssemblyVersion by hand." />
  </Target>

</Project>
